"""Questions and direct explanations for the revised teaching edition."""
QUESTIONS={}
def add(n,raw):
    QUESTIONS[n]=[tuple(x.strip().replace(' ¶ ','\n\n') for x in row.split(' || ')) for row in raw.strip().splitlines() if row.strip()]
    assert len(QUESTIONS[n])==8 and all(len(x)==2 for x in QUESTIONS[n]),n

add(2, '''Opening a feed appears to be one action. Why might it create many requests and writes? || The browser can fetch images and send analytics in addition to its feed request. Publishing can also create references for many followers. Count the logical action separately from the physical work at each boundary, or storage and transfer estimates will miss that amplification.
Requests keep arriving faster, but successful completions stay at the same rate. What happens next? || Once a limiting resource cannot complete work faster, additional requests wait or are rejected. Compare offered traffic, useful completions, errors, and queue growth. A steady completion rate can coexist with a rapidly worsening user wait; it does not establish that increasing demand is being handled.
How can a 19.9 ms mean hide a request that took one second? || In the example, 99 requests take 10 ms and one takes 1,000 ms. Their total is 1,990 ms, so the mean is 19.9 ms. The calculation is correct, but it compresses the distribution. Inspect tail observations and percentiles to understand the slow user's experience.
Which requests belong in an event-based error budget? || Define the eligible population and good outcome before calculating the budget. At 99.9% success over one million eligible events, 1,000 bad events are allowed. Excluding the failures the objective was meant to track would make the number misleading. A time-based objective uses a different denominator.
When is L = λW useful for estimating work in progress? || Use stable long-run averages within the same boundary and consistent units. At 500 operations/s and 0.04 seconds mean residence, L is twenty operations. Include relevant waiting in residence time. The relation does not predict p99 or show that a queue with sustained excess arrivals will become stable.
A traffic spike ends before new instances can start. What protects the service? || Existing headroom and admission controls must handle the interval before scaling takes effect. Bound waiting and protect downstream capacity. Measure the spike's duration as well as height, and account for retries. Adding capacity after the demand has passed cannot undo the delay and overload users already experienced.
Only 80% of a job can run in parallel. What ideal speedup can four workers give? || The sequential fraction takes 0.2 of the old time; the parallel portion ideally takes 0.8/4 = 0.2. Total time is 0.4, giving 2.5× speedup. Coordination can reduce the actual gain further. Four workers cannot eliminate the fixed sequential part.
Why can requiring fifty parallel calls increase tail latency? || The request waits for its slowest required result. If each call independently has a 1% slow probability, at least one is slow with probability 1−0.99⁵⁰, about 39.5%. Independence is an assumption; shared dependencies can correlate delays. Parallel initiation does not remove the wait for the last result.''')
add(3, '''A server listens on port 443. Why might a remote caller still fail to use it? || Binding, routing, firewall rules, and the return path can prevent communication even when a process listens. If communication succeeds, the caller still needs the required identity and permissions. Reachability and authorisation answer different questions, so checking the port establishes neither the whole network path nor access rights.
What does the application receive from TCP, UDP, and QUIC? || TCP provides an ordered reliable byte stream, so the application defines its own message framing. UDP provides datagrams without that stream contract. QUIC implements secure streams over UDP with its own transport rules. Choose using required behaviour and deployment support rather than assuming a particular label always means faster delivery.
Why does changing DNS not move every client immediately? || Clients may keep a cached answer until refresh, including negative caching behaviour. Existing connections may continue without another lookup at all. The authoritative record can therefore change before actual traffic does. Check resolver caching and connection lifetimes together when evaluating failover.
Which access checks can a gateway perform, and which need domain information? || A gateway can validate appropriate boundary credentials and apply coarse admission or routing. Whether a caller owns payment 42 requires trustworthy resource and membership information. Keep that domain rule enforced where it is known; an authenticated request at the edge is not automatically authorised for every object.
What work does streaming save compared with polling, and what work does it add? || Streaming can avoid repeated empty polls. It keeps connections and buffers alive and needs heartbeat, reconnect, and recovery rules. A client returning after disconnection may still need retained history or a fresh snapshot. The live transport alone does not preserve every required business update.
The server commits a write, but the client times out. What can the client conclude? || It knows that it did not receive a timely reply, not that the write failed. The committed effect can exist. Recover by the operation's stable identity or status contract rather than blindly creating another effect. Bound retries and keep uncertainty visible until there is evidence of the outcome.
Why might a cold HTTPS request take longer than a warm request with the same server work? || The cold path can add DNS, transport, and security setup. In the illustrative model those stages add 20+40+40 = 100 ms before the same 55 ms request/work stage. Reuse removes only steps actually reused. Protocol, caching, and overlap can change this assumed path.
A database pool is full while CPU is low. What should you inspect first? || Measure connection-acquisition waits, hold times, lock waits, and idle transactions. Callers may keep connections while waiting on I/O or external services. More connections can increase downstream contention. Find what holds the existing pool before treating the symptom as a need for more CPU or a larger pool.''')
add(4, '''How do processes and threads differ from modules in the source code? || A process owns runtime resources and an address space; threads execute within it and commonly share its heap. Modules organise code. Several modules can share the same process and stop together in a crash. Source separation alone does not create independent resource or failure boundaries.
Why can an async function still block other requests? || It can call a blocking library operation or perform long computation on the event-loop thread. Other ready tasks then wait for that thread. Inspect the driver and actual execution path. Async syntax does not by itself change underlying calls into nonblocking operations or supply additional CPU cores.
Can a one-core program be concurrent without executing tasks simultaneously? || Yes. Task A can wait while the core executes B, so both are in progress over overlapping periods. That is concurrency. Parallelism requires simultaneous execution resources. Interleaving can still create races on one core, because a task can be interrupted between the read and write of a compound update.
Why might atomic fields fail to make a transfer between two balances safe? || Each field operation may be indivisible while the relationship between the two values is temporarily or permanently wrong. The invariant spans both balances. Protect the combined operation with the appropriate atomic boundary or coordination, rather than assuming several separate atomic assignments form one transaction.
Why does an earlier timestamp not prove another thread sees an update? || Visibility follows the language memory model and its synchronisation rules. A correctly used lock, channel, or suitable atomic publication can establish the required happens-before relationship. Wall-clock order alone supplies no such relationship. Use the actual runtime's guarantees rather than an intuition about elapsed time.
A and B read counter zero, then each writes one. Where was the increment lost? || Both calculate from the same earlier value. B's write replaces A's result instead of adding to it. Protect the entire read–modify–write operation. Locking only the final assignment, or using separate locks for each caller, leaves the shared invariant unprotected.
One callback computes for two seconds on a single event loop. What happens to ready tasks? || They cannot execute on that loop until the callback yields or finishes. Completed I/O may make tasks ready without giving them CPU time. Keep loop turns bounded or move appropriate computation to a bounded execution resource. More queued async tasks do not make the long callback run in parallel.
Jobs arrive at 150/s and complete at 100/s. Does a larger queue fix the problem? || It stores fifty more waiting jobs each second until its limit is reached. More space delays rejection but does not change the service rate. Reduce admitted work or add useful capacity, bound queue age, and return clear overload outcomes before waiting becomes useless or resources are exhausted.''')
add(5, '''When can composition avoid many subclasses? || A payment service can combine a gateway and a fraud policy independently instead of creating one subclass for each combination. The collaborators need clear contracts and wiring. Inheritance remains appropriate when specialisation preserves the caller's expected behaviour. Similar fields alone do not establish that relationship.
What must a payment gateway contract explain beyond charge(amount)? || Specify currency, amount units, operation identity, possible states, and error meanings. In particular, a timeout can leave an attempted charge uncertain. Callers need that distinction to recover safely. Returning an undifferentiated Boolean hides information needed to distinguish decline, approved effect, and lost response.
Why does applying SOLID not require an interface for every class? || The principles help protect responsibilities, substitution, and useful dependency boundaries. An extra interface is valuable when it hides meaningful variation or knowledge. If it only forwards every call, it can add navigation and maintenance without improving behaviour. Evaluate a concrete change or test need rather than maximising interface count.
How is Payment 42 different from Money(USD,100)? || Payment 42 retains its identity as its status changes, so it is an entity. Equal Money values can represent the same amount and currency without separate identity, making Money a value object. Both need valid rules; combining different currencies requires an explicit conversion policy rather than arbitrary arithmetic.
How can pure functions and external payment effects work together? || A pure decision function takes explicit state and command data and returns decisions or requested actions. A separate interpreter performs storage and provider calls. This makes decisions testable, while the effect path still needs durability, authorisation, duplicate handling, and reconciliation. Pure local calculation does not make a remote charge atomic.
Why ask an account to reserve an amount rather than overwrite its balance from outside? || The account owner can keep the rule and coordinated change together. External read/compare/write logic duplicates policy and can race with other writers. Reporting a balance is still a valid query. The owner must enforce the update at the actual shared storage boundary; merely naming a method reserve does not supply atomicity.
A mock confirms every expected method call. What could still be wrong? || The final payment state, stored transaction, encoding, or timeout recovery can still violate the contract. Prefer tests of required outcomes and invariants, and integration evidence for real boundaries. A mock can make a specific path easy to exercise but cannot prove the behaviour of the component it replaced.
A refactor changes provider timeout into decline. Has it preserved behaviour? || No. Decline says the operation was rejected, while timeout can mean the effect happened but its reply was lost. Callers may retry differently and create duplicates. A structural refactor should preserve this meaning, with tests covering approved, declined, uncertain, and storage-failure outcomes.''')
add(6, '''Can a constructor use DI while the service still depends on provider details? || Yes. Passing a concrete SDK is dependency injection, but the service may still consume its request types and exceptions. Dependency inversion concerns which interface policy relies on. An application-owned contract can separate the provider meaning; construction syntax alone cannot establish that separation.
Is a singleton necessarily one object across every host? || No. The lifetime is scoped to the specified container or application arrangement. Each process may have its own singleton. Sharing one object also does not establish safe concurrent use. State its ownership, lifetime, and allowed callers instead of inferring global uniqueness from the name.
Why must a factory say who owns the resource it returns? || A returned connection may be new and caller-owned, shared, or managed by a scope. Closing a shared object can break other users; never closing an owned object can leak capacity. Creation and borrowing are distinct from disposal responsibility, so include that responsibility in the contract.
Why can injecting a container leave dependencies hidden? || Application methods can still resolve arbitrary services at runtime instead of naming what they require. This behaves like a service locator. Passing focused contracts makes needs visible at construction or call time. Keep broad resolution near a deliberate integration or composition boundary.
How can a factory and DI cooperate? || A service can receive a narrow sender factory, then ask it for the appropriate channel during an operation. Injection supplies the factory; the factory performs selection or creation. Define the returned sender's lifetime so the caller knows whether it owns disposal or merely borrows a shared instance.
A singleton stores a request-scoped unit of work. What happens after the request ends? || The unit of work may be disposed while the singleton still holds and uses it. This captive dependency breaks the intended lifetime and can retain request context. Supply a currently valid per-operation dependency or appropriate scope/factory rather than hiding the problem with a global lookup.
Two callers trigger lazy initialisation together. What rules are needed? || Coordinate creation and safe publication so they do not use partially initialised or unintended duplicate state. Decide how failure is reported and whether later callers retry. A supported lazy primitive or lock can help. Essential resources may be clearer to initialise before advertising readiness.
Why can leaking one connection per failed request stop healthy requests later? || Each leak reduces the pool's usable capacity. Eventually new requests cannot borrow a connection even if their own code would succeed. Cleanup must cover failure and cancellation as well as success. Timely release of external resources cannot be assumed from memory garbage collection alone.''')
add(7, '''What makes a monolith modular if it has one deployment? || Its modules have clear responsibilities, controlled interfaces, and data ownership. Orders should use a payment contract rather than alter private payment tables. One deployment keeps calls local but does not prevent useful boundaries. Enforce important dependencies through review or appropriate checks.
Why can dependency arrows point differently from runtime calls? || A service can depend on an application-owned repository interface, while a database adapter implements it. At runtime, calls execute the supplied adapter. The source arrow describes knowledge or implementation relationships, not necessarily the physical direction of a message or a separate network service.
Why can catalog Product and fulfilment Item be different valid models? || They describe different business work. Catalog may care about descriptions and categories, while fulfilment tracks units and locations. A bounded context identifies where each meaning applies. Translate between them deliberately rather than forcing both into one schema that obscures their rules.
How should an aggregate boundary follow the rule it protects? || Group state that must change atomically under a root controlling those changes. An order's line values and total may need one consistency boundary. Making the entire shop one aggregate adds contention. Cross-aggregate work needs explicit coordination rather than an assumption that object references create a shared transaction.
What should an architecture fitness check preserve? || A meaningful required property, such as allowed dependencies, migration compatibility, or tenant isolation. It should detect erosion of that reasoned boundary without freezing incidental class names. Update the check when the architecture decision legitimately changes, and keep evidence within the check's actual scope.
An order commits, then the process dies before publishing its event. What is missing? || Publication intent existed only in memory or a separate unsafe step. An outbox can commit that intent with the order so a relay can resume later. The relay may publish again after losing its own progress record, so consumers still need stable event identity and duplicate handling.
Five services share writable tables and always release together. What independence is missing? || They cannot change their contracts or stored representation independently and can remain tightly coupled operationally. The network split adds failure and diagnosis work without delivering much independent deployment. Clarify ownership and boundaries, or consider a modular consolidation where appropriate.
When would extracting a module into a service be justified? || Look for a stable capability with a concrete scaling, fault-isolation, or team-ownership need, plus the ability to operate it. Compare the benefit with network failures, versioning, data coordination, security, and on-call costs. A rapidly changing model alone is not evidence that more process boundaries will help.''')
add(8, '''Why give a refund its own identity and status? || A refund can be requested later, authorised separately, and complete or fail independently of the original payment. Its own identity supports amount checks, retries, and investigation. Treating it as an untracked toggle on paid loses the history and uncertainty needed for recovery.
What does a keyset cursor improve, and what does it leave unresolved? || It continues after a stable ordered key rather than skipping a shifting row count, avoiding some duplicates and omissions from insertions before an offset. It also needs validation and query/tenant scope. Changing records can still affect results; the cursor does not automatically provide one consistent snapshot.
What makes an idempotency key more than a string in a request? || Durable state binds owner, key, payload fingerprint, progress, and result with suitable uniqueness and atomicity. Identical retries recover the same operation; different content conflicts. Retention and provider recovery rules matter too. A separate unprotected lookup and insert can race or forget an external effect.
Why does signing in not grant access to GET /payments/42? || Authentication identifies the caller. Authorisation must still check this caller's permitted action on payment 42 using trusted membership and resource scope. Guess-resistant IDs and gateway authentication do not establish ownership. Apply the rule on every relevant query and effect path.
How can removing an old event decoder break recovery after a successful release? || Historical logs and backups can still contain that version. A restoring or delayed consumer needs to interpret it even when current traffic uses only the newest shape. Keep supported historical samples and transformations, including meanings, defaults, and unknown values, for the required replay horizon.
Does 201 Created for a pending payment mean money was captured? || No. It can mean the payment resource was created successfully while its financial operation remains pending. The body and operation contract describe that later state. HTTP response semantics do not make a provider effect part of the local resource transaction.
Four layers each allow three attempts. How much downstream work can one request cause? || Up to 3⁴ = 81 attempts in the nested worst case. Early success can reduce actual work, but failure can trigger amplification precisely when capacity is scarce. Use an overall retry budget and deadline, and safe operation identity for effects.
A webhook effect commits but its reply is lost. What should redelivery do? || Recognise the stable event identity and avoid repeating the established local effect. Persist recognition and effect together when that is the protected boundary. An unrelated external call needs its own protocol. The sender may legitimately retry because it did not observe acknowledgment.''')
add(9, '''What must be decided before choosing a storage family? || State the important reads and changes, accepted-result boundary, invariants, retention, and supported failures. Identify the authoritative copy and recovery procedure. A product category does not establish configured isolation, durable acknowledgment, or usable backups, so verify the selected implementation against those needs.
Why is a similar embedding result not proof of truth or permission? || An embedding is a derived numerical representation used to find candidates. Approximate similarity can miss items or retrieve imperfect matches. Preserve source versions, verify required evidence, and enforce access. A vector score describes its retrieval relationship, not factual correctness or authorisation.
When is storing an address twice correct? || A profile can hold the current address while a completed order retains the address actually used at purchase. Those records represent different facts. A maintained cache of the current address has another meaning and needs an update policy. State whether repetition preserves history or tracks a changing source.
Why is data ownership broader than creating a table? || The owner defines allowed changes and access contracts and is responsible for schema evolution, retention, incidents, and recovery. If every service writes the table directly, they share release and transaction assumptions despite separate repositories. Ownership needs an enforcement mechanism and operating responsibility.
What work appears when a second storage engine is added? || Backup and restoration, access policy, schema changes, monitoring, expertise, and reconciliation now cross another boundary. If it is a derived search store, also manage lag and rebuilding. Compare the new capability with that ownership cost rather than assuming a specialised engine is automatically a net improvement.
Why do more cold partitions not solve one very busy conversation partition? || The hot conversation's requests still reach its owner. Extra unrelated capacity does not divide that key's work. Measure whether the pressure comes from size, reads, writes, or ordering, then choose supported splitting, read distribution, or a changed data model that preserves the required semantics.
Search still holds a deleted private document. What must the system do? || Enforce the required access and deletion policy when serving, even while asynchronous cleanup catches up. Carry source identity and version so old updates cannot recreate an invalid visible record. Track physical cleanup and derived-copy lifecycle rather than treating a successful delete in the source as completion everywhere.
What prevents an older backup from restoring deleted content into live service? || Recovery must apply the relevant deletion history and lifecycle rules before exposing restored copies. Track raw and derived data, including indexes and replay paths. A narrowly retained marker can help prevent resurrection, but has its own minimisation and retention needs.''')
add(10, '''Why might range and equality queries need different indexes? || An ordered tree can locate a range and continue through neighbouring keys. A hash structure targets equality under its implementation and does not supply ordered traversal. Compare predicates, output size, locality, write maintenance, and engine support rather than choosing only from an abstract lookup complexity.
Why does an ACID transaction not automatically preserve every business rule? || The application and constraints must express those rules. The engine can atomically commit a wrongly calculated transfer. Isolation and durability also depend on the selected contract. ACID supplies mechanisms and properties, not knowledge of what the business intended the amount or relationship to mean.
Why is MVCC alone insufficient to claim serializability? || Versions can give readers a stable snapshot while concurrent transactions change different rows and violate a shared condition. The two-doctor write-skew example illustrates it. Check the isolation and conflict-detection rules of the engine, not merely whether it retains old row versions.
Why state the engine and statement behaviour when discussing isolation? || Identically named levels can provide different visibility and conflict behaviour. For example, PostgreSQL 18 ordinary Read Committed uses statement snapshots, while its stronger levels have other rules. Handle supported retryable failures for the whole transaction using fresh assumptions and bounded attempts.
How do serializability and linearizability differ? || Serializability makes transactions equivalent to some serial order. Linearizability constrains operations by real-time precedence. Strict serializability combines transaction ordering with real-time requirements. Neither automatically includes an unrelated provider call outside the database's transaction boundary.
Two doctors each see the other on call and both go off duty. What rule was missed? || The invariant spans both rows: at least one doctor must remain on call. Separate row updates can each look acceptable under the old snapshot while jointly violating it. Use suitable serialization, locking, or a shared conflict point that protects the complete rule.
A conditional seat update changes zero rows. What does that mean? || The expected version or availability condition no longer matched. Treat it as conflict, re-read, and reconsider the business action. Do not report a reservation merely because the statement executed without a transport error. Success depends on the guarded state transition actually occurring.
A backup job succeeded. What recovery evidence is still needed? || Restore it in a usable environment with the required keys, schema, credentials, and logs. Check application invariants and measure the full recovery time. File creation or checksum success alone does not establish that the service can recover its required state and start serving correctly.''')
add(11, '''Why does a working set matter more than the full history size for caching? || Reuse concerns the data active in the chosen interval. A small cache can serve a much larger history if most requests reuse a small portion. If the active set exceeds it, eviction and reload can churn. Include bytes, object size, scan traffic, and admission behaviour as well as entry count.
How can cache-aside reinsert an old value after invalidation? || A reader fetches version one, then pauses. A writer commits version two and invalidates the key. The reader resumes and inserts its old value. Ordering a source write before cache deletion alone does not prevent this history; stronger freshness needs suitable version or coordination rules.
Why can a one-minute TTL serve data older than one minute? || TTL starts when the cache inserts the value. If that value already lagged the source by five minutes, another minute of residence can make it six minutes old. Expiry and source freshness are different measurements. Choose the policy according to the operation's tolerated staleness.
For A,B,C,A,D at capacity three, what do ideal LRU and FIFO evict? || Ideal LRU refreshes A's recency, leaving B oldest when D arrives, so it evicts B. FIFO preserves insertion order and evicts A. This fixture explains their difference; real engines can approximate policies and use byte limits or separate expiration rules.
When might removing a cache improve the design? || When reuse saves less than lookup, encoding, memory, and maintenance cost, or when a simpler source improvement meets the requirement with fewer freshness risks. Test the empty and unavailable paths. A warm cache can hide an origin-capacity problem, while a wrongly scoped cache can create a data leak.
A cache restart sends misses to the origin. How can that load be controlled? || Combine same-key loading where useful, bound refresh workers, apply origin admission, and rebuild gradually. Negative caching and expiry jitter address particular patterns. Do not let every new instance independently scan the whole source. Protect recovery capacity as well as normal cache-hit traffic.
A product is created just after “not found” is cached. What should the policy explain? || How long the negative entry may hide the new product and whether creation invalidates it. Distinguish genuine absence from temporary source failure and authorisation denial. Keys and lifetimes must prevent one tenant's response from misleading another.
Refresh keeps failing while stale data is served. What limit is needed? || Define fresh, stale-permitted, and unusable ages or another explicit degradation bound. Serving must stop or change once the allowed stale condition ends. Some descriptions tolerate old values, but correctness-critical operations need their authoritative checks. Do not silently extend stale use forever.''')
add(12, '''Why can adding application servers fail to improve throughput? || They can send more work to the same saturated database, disk, lock, or provider. Identify the constrained path through waits and useful completions first. Extra independent compute helps when compute is the limit and downstream capacity remains available; it is not a universal response to a slow service.
What becomes harder when a dataset is sharded? || Routing, ownership movement, cross-shard queries, uniqueness, and transactions can require additional protocols. A tenant key may preserve useful locality and leave one large tenant hot. Choose the key and recovery procedure from the actual access and invariant needs rather than from total row count alone.
What does stable hashing change, and what does it not balance? || It limits some reassignment when owners change. In rendezvous hashing, adding D moves only keys newly won by D in the model. It does not transfer bytes, synchronise membership, or divide one popular key's traffic. Key distribution and request popularity are separate measurements.
Why distinguish received and applied replica progress? || A replica can receive log bytes without yet applying them to the state it serves. Required persistence is another milestone. These positions help explain stale reads and decide safe promotion. “The replica has the update” is too vague unless it names the stage relevant to the claim.
Why compare scaling costs under the same failure objectives? || A cheaper healthy-state arrangement may have no capacity after one node fails or may lose more acknowledged data. That is a different service. Include surviving capacity, copies, coordination, transfer, monitoring, and operating work when comparing equal useful outcomes.
One partition receives 70% of writes. What should guide the fix? || Find whether its pressure comes from many divisible keys, one hot entity, large records, or a serialised invariant. Splitting a range can help divisible work; more cold partitions cannot split a single key automatically. Choose a change that preserves query and ownership semantics while changing the measured pressure.
How can scaling application replicas make a database outage worse? || Each replica may create another pool, multiplying total connections and competing work. During retries, more instances can add attempted demand without useful capacity. Bound aggregate admission and investigate connection hold time and contention before increasing every pool or instance count.
Two replicas share one host. Which failure do they not survive? || That host's loss can stop both. They may tolerate some individual process failures while sharing disk, power, configuration, or credentials. Map independent failure domains and actual takeover capacity instead of treating two process boxes as two fully independent copies.''')
add(13, '''Why do acknowledged upload parts not prove the completed file is correct? || The parts still need correct assembly, identity, and completion verification. Repeated parts or a changed client source can produce another result. Check final size and integrity under the storage protocol. A provider-specific entity tag is not universally a checksum of the final content.
Why does a content hash not authorise a download? || The digest identifies bytes under the algorithm's assumptions. It says nothing about the requester's permission to those bytes or their safety. Bind object access to ownership and policy, and scope deduplication carefully where exposing existence could leak information across tenants.
What does an inverted index record for “blue car” and “blue bike”? || With documents D1 and D2, the blue posting list contains both IDs, while car contains D1 and bike contains D2. Tokenisation affects the recorded terms. Versions and permission rules are additional metadata needed to keep this derived lookup usable and safe.
What can go wrong between a snapshot and its CDC stream? || Changes made while the snapshot is copied can be missed or applied incorrectly unless the handoff uses a defined consistent position. Preserve identities, ordering information, and schema support, and handle repetition. Transporting changes does not automatically give every destination query the source's transaction view.
What provenance makes an extracted field explainable? || Name the exact file version and source region, transform or extractor version, relevant parameters, and review decision. The references should locate actual evidence. Confidence alone is not provenance or accuracy. Retain the required evidence under an appropriate privacy and lifecycle policy.
Updates and deletes continue during reindexing. What must happen before serving switches? || Apply changes from a defined snapshot position through a validated catch-up boundary, including removals. Check identities, versions, permissions, and useful output before selecting the new generation. Keep a compatible rollback path. Matching document counts alone cannot establish readiness.
An event arrives three minutes late. Which time policy matters? || Distinguish its source event time from handling time. The window needs an allowed-lateness and correction rule, with clear provisional or final results. A watermark estimates progress under assumptions; it does not prove no older event can ever arrive.
Why is deleting only the PDF insufficient? || Extracted text, thumbnails, indexes, logs, and recoverable copies can retain its information. Track the required deletion work across those paths and prevent old workers or replay from republishing it. Recovery must also apply the relevant deletion history before exposing older restored data.''')
add(14, '''Why is a provider charge with a lost reply a partial failure? || The external action happened while the local caller's observation did not complete. A local rollback cannot undo it. Record uncertainty and reconcile using a stable operation reference. Treating the timeout as a definite decline can lead to another charge.
What does Lamport ordering establish? || Causally ordered events receive increasing Lamport values under the algorithm. A lower value alone does not prove that event caused the higher-valued event. Vector clocks can identify concurrency under their participant model, but neither clock supplies a business conflict resolution or consensus decision by itself.
Which real-time rule must a linearizable register preserve? || A completed write must precede a read that starts afterward in the allowed atomic history. Without an intervening write, that read cannot return an overwritten earlier value. Overlapping operations may have several legal orders. A timeout can still correspond to an effect whose result the client did not receive.
Why is CAP not a permanent choice of any two attractive qualities? || It concerns defined atomic-consistency and availability properties in the presence of partitions. During lost communication, decide which operations wait, reject, or offer an explicitly weaker result. Ordinary nonpartitioned coordination and latency need further analysis. The formal terms should not be equated loosely with product adjectives.
Can a system preserve safety while being useless? || Yes. Rejecting every booking avoids selling a seat twice but makes no accepted valid booking progress. State both the forbidden outcome and the progress required under explicit environmental assumptions. Recovery must restore useful progress without violating exclusive ownership.
A session sees version ten, then version nine. What guarantee failed? || Its reads moved backward, violating monotonic reads if that guarantee was promised. Session progress may need routing or version tracking, including after failover or device changes. A sticky instance alone does not establish the behaviour for every transition.
Why does R=2,W=2,N=3 not prove linearizability? || The sets intersect, so a read contacts at least one member of the write set. The protocol still needs version interpretation, concurrent-write ordering, durable evidence, membership, and read rules. A set relationship is one ingredient, not a complete legal-history argument.
An old leader resumes after a replacement takes over. Where must stale effects be stopped? || At the resource that accepts them, using a suitable authority protocol such as atomically checked fencing epochs. A local timer cannot stop the old process. Test pauses and delayed messages; a clean process kill hides the resumed-owner history.''')
add(15, '''What must “synchronous replication” say about acknowledgment? || Specify the required replicas and whether their replies mean receipt, durable storage, or application. Those milestones support different recovery and read guarantees. Waiting adds latency and can block during communication failure. Test promotion under the same acknowledgment contract the application promises.
Why must replicated command application be deterministic? || Replicas applying the same ordered commands must obtain equivalent state. Independently choosing time or random IDs can make results differ. Supply such decisions consistently in the agreed input or use the protocol's supported mechanism. External side effects remain separately identified and protected.
Why does Raft require more than counting two acknowledgments? || Votes, log eligibility, durable state, terms, and commit rules work together. Direct majority-based commitment is restricted to current-term entries; earlier history is protected through those rules. An arbitrary older entry on two nodes is not a replacement safety argument. Reads and membership also need their specified mechanisms.
Why is an abrupt switch to a disjoint voting group unsafe? || Old and new majorities could independently accept incompatible histories with no shared evidence. Use the supported membership protocol, such as Raft's joint-consensus transition, and catch new members up appropriately. Preserve identities and test interruption while the configuration changes.
Why can convergent replicas still violate a stock rule? || A merge can deterministically include all updates while their combined effect takes stock below zero. Convergence describes agreement under the type's rules; it does not enforce arbitrary cross-update invariants. Choose semantics that fit the domain or introduce the coordination needed for exclusive allocation.
Worker 7 resumes after worker 8 owns the attempt. What enforcement is needed? || The protected receiver must atomically compare authority and reject the stale epoch before applying its effect. Creating tokens only in the coordinator does not protect an external store that ignores them. Local workflow checks cover local progress, not every remote operation automatically.
A pause creates a false failure suspicion. What must remain correct? || The authority and safety protocol must prevent conflicting accepted decisions even when detection is wrong. Detection tuning affects speed and false suspicion, while election, persistence, and fencing determine which effects are valid. A missing heartbeat cannot be treated as proof that the old process no longer runs.
What must happen before a repaired old primary accepts writes again? || It must rejoin under current authority and reconcile or rebuild its history as required. Fence stale writes and account for replica lag and the acknowledged-data policy. Test failback as well as promotion; restoring traffic to an old owner prematurely can reintroduce conflict after the apparent outage ended.''')
add(16, '''Why cannot an absent ephemeral subscriber replay missed history automatically? || Its subscription may retain nothing while it is disconnected. Recovery requires retained records and a way to track progress, such as a log position under the chosen system. Check the actual broker policy rather than assuming every pub/sub product provides durable replay.
How does acknowledgment placement change crash behaviour? || Acknowledging before a required effect can lose work after a crash. Applying the effect first can repeat work if acknowledgment is lost. Protect repetition at the relevant boundary, such as an inbox and local effect in one transaction. Neither position alone covers an unrelated external provider.
What can an exactly-once stream transaction actually cover? || The records and progress atomically coupled by its supported protocol and fault assumptions. An arbitrary email server is outside that boundary unless another mechanism covers it. Name the effect and identity scope. Repeated execution can still produce one local relevant result through idempotency without execution starting only once.
Why can an outbox relay publish the same event twice? || It can publish successfully, then crash before saving that it sent the event. Recovery sees the pending intent and publishes again. Stable event identity and consumer duplicate handling address this gap. The original database transaction records the obligation; it does not include the entire network delivery.
Why is saga compensation not always an undo? || A refund or reservation release is another action that may fail, happen later, or have different fees and consequences from the original. Persist compensation progress and reconcile uncertainty. Some effects cannot be perfectly reversed, so the business must define an acceptable recovery outcome.
An effect commits and the message is delivered again. How can one local update be preserved? || Store the unique event identity and update in the same local transaction. A duplicate then observes the established identity and skips repeating the effect. Retain it for the supported replay horizon. Substituting an external call for the local update requires its own protocol.
A worker keeps running after its lease expires. What protects later effects? || A current-epoch check can reject stale local completion, while the actual effect receiver needs appropriate fencing or duplicate identity. The expired lease itself cannot stop execution. Distinguish effects already accepted before replacement from later stale attempts, and reconcile what remains uncertain.
Why not resend an uncertain payment as a new operation? || The provider may already have charged. Query by stable operation identity and repair local progress, or use the provider's supported safe retry. A new identity can produce another financial effect. Reconciliation is part of the recovery protocol rather than evidence that the original intent never ran.''')
add(17, '''Why are two replicas not necessarily enough surviving capacity? || Their independence and spare resources matter. Two instances each at 70% cannot absorb the other's full traffic after one fails. Plan headroom or a permitted reduced service, with valid state and tested traffic movement. Shared configuration or storage can also fail both together.
Why include pool acquisition in the overall deadline? || A request spends useful lifetime waiting before it starts its database work. Giving each stage a fresh full timeout can let it run long after the caller's deadline. Propagate remaining time and clean up appropriately. A timeout still does not prove that a remote effect never occurred.
What does a circuit breaker help, and what remains unresolved? || It can stop repeated futile calls and resource waits while allowing bounded recovery probes. It does not repair the dependency or decide a valid business fallback. Define failures and probe limits. Returning a fabricated payment approval would preserve neither correctness nor a useful recovery contract.
How can separate worker pools still share a failure bottleneck? || They may exhaust the same database, memory, network, or credential dependency. Partition the resources through which harm can spread, and measure each budget. Separate names or threads do not establish independent downstream capacity. Excess separation also has idle-capacity costs.
How do RTO and RPO differ? || RTO concerns how soon required service should recover. RPO concerns acceptable data-loss exposure in time. Restoring at 12:20 after a noon incident and recovering data only through 11:55 gives different twenty-minute and five-minute measurements. Test the full recovery procedure against both objectives.
How should an end-to-end design limit a retry storm? || Use one overall attempt budget and deadline, bounded admission, safe operation identity, and resource isolation. Backoff with jitter spreads timing but does not reduce allowed attempts by itself. Track useful intents and completions separately from attempts, and leave capacity available for recovery.
Can a default cached balance replace an unavailable ledger? || Only a contract explicitly permitting that particular observation could justify a display. It cannot invent authority to approve a financial action. A help page and a ledger lookup have different meanings. Define freshness, duration, permissions, and allowed degraded operations instead of treating any returned value as a safe fallback.
Why not send traffic straight back to a repaired primary? || It may have stale or incompatible history and no current authority. Rejoin, fence, reconcile, and verify capacity and routing according to the protocol. Existing connections and cached addresses can complicate the transition. Failback needs testing just as failover does.''')
add(18, '''Why is an internal network address not proof of a caller's identity? || Network location establishes a route or reachability, not necessarily the actor's identity or permission. A compromised internal component can still send harmful requests. Validate the relevant credentials and resource policy at explicit boundaries, including trusted forwarding configuration rather than arbitrary supplied headers.
How do OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect differ in purpose? || OAuth concerns delegated access authorisation. OpenID Connect adds an identity layer with defined assertions and validation. An arbitrary access token is not automatically a login assertion. Use the intended flow and reviewed validation for issuer, audience, token type, and required context.
Why use purpose-built password hashing rather than a fast hash? || Offline attackers can try guesses cheaply against fast hashes. Password hashing adds salts and deliberately expensive work under a suitable algorithm and parameters. Use maintained libraries and current guidance, with migration metadata and capacity checks. Recovery and online verification also need separate abuse protection.
Which paths need tenant isolation beyond the database query? || Caches, task messages, worker reads and writes, object capabilities, search, exports, telemetry, and administrative tools can all carry private data. Preserve trusted scope and current resource relationships throughout. A correct query cannot compensate for a public object link or a cache key that omits tenant identity.
Does an artifact's valid signature prove its code is safe? || It proves the checked signing relationship and integrity under the scheme's assumptions. The signer or build inputs can still be compromised, or the code can be vulnerable. Control dependencies, build privileges, provenance, deployment access, and revocation alongside signature verification.
A tenant A administrator guesses a tenant B document ID. What denies access? || Resource-specific authorisation using trustworthy membership and ownership must reject it on every relevant path. The tenant-admin role is scoped, not global. Difficult identifiers and a successfully authenticated session do not supply the missing permission.
A backup survives but its only decryption key is destroyed. What went wrong? || Recovery depended on a key that did not survive the protected failure. Backup testing must include key versions, credentials, schema, and the actual application restore path. Preserved ciphertext alone does not establish usable recoverable data.
Membership is revoked after an export is queued. Where must that policy be enforced? || At each later boundary required by the chosen revocation contract, including execution and publication or download. The original request's valid permission may no longer suffice. Give queued work trusted context but recheck current state where needed, and track denied or cancelled progress durably.''')
add(19, '''How do logs, metrics, and traces complement one another? || Metrics describe populations and trends, traces connect one execution's work and waits, and logs record events with detailed context. Use stable identities to relate appropriate evidence. Sampling, dropped records, and privacy limits affect all three, so no single signal automatically tells the complete story.
Why is a trace ID not an access credential? || It labels diagnostic context, which may originate from an untrusted caller and does not prove identity or resource permission. Validate or replace context according to policy. Authentication and authorisation must use their intended evidence, while trace propagation helps investigate the resulting operation.
Why can low CPU coexist with severe request delay? || Requests can be waiting for connections, locks, storage, or a slow remote call. RED can show rising durations while USE identifies saturation of the constrained resource. Inspect the path before buying CPU; idle compute does not free an occupied connection pool.
What is tenfold budget burn for a 99.9% success target? || The allowed bad-event rate is 0.1%. An observed 1% bad-event rate spends budget ten times as fast for the defined eligible population. Keep the measurement window and denominator clear, and segment critical work where a large overall count could hide its harm.
Why avoid unique request IDs as ordinary metric labels? || Each new value can create another time series, making cardinality and storage grow without a practical bound. Use bounded dimensions for metrics and put detailed operation identities in appropriate logs or traces. Retention and sampling should match the diagnostic purpose.
A five-second request waits 4.6 seconds for a connection. Where should investigation focus? || Pool wait accounts for 92% of that request's time. Examine hold times, locks, queueing, and population evidence before assuming query execution or CPU is the problem. One trace suggests a mechanism to test; it does not explain why the pool is full by itself.
Serving recovers but charges remain uncertain. Why is recovery incomplete? || Users can reach the API while business outcomes still disagree with the provider or lack postings. Track those operations through reconciliation and safe final states. Closing an infrastructure alert does not establish that every accepted financial intent is resolved.
HTTP 202 is fast while jobs wait for hours. Which indicators are missing? || Measure submission-to-start and submission-to-required-outcome age, stage queues, attempts, and final useful completion. Follow stable operation identity beyond the HTTP trace. Acceptance latency describes only one boundary and can remain excellent while the promised asynchronous service stops progressing.''')
add(20, '''What important boundary differs between a VM and a typical container? || A VM supplies a guest operating system on virtualised hardware; containers commonly share a host kernel while isolating processes. Both need resource, identity, network, storage, and image management. Check actual isolation and shared failure domains rather than treating packaging as a complete security or reliability guarantee.
How can a control-plane outage differ from a serving outage? || Existing workloads may keep serving while new placement or configuration changes cannot proceed. Replacement may need a dependency that current requests do not use. Draw control and data paths separately and define cached-configuration validity. Avoid unnecessary fresh control decisions in each request.
Why must a canary receive representative traffic? || A candidate exposed only to cheap reads can appear healthy while failing the expensive checkout path. Select cohorts and indicators that exercise the release risks, with stop conditions and sufficient observations. A small exposure is useful only when it tests behaviour relevant to the broader rollout.
Why can an old executable fail as a rollback? || New code may have written data or a schema that the old reader cannot understand. Expand-and-contract preserves an overlap period before obsolete state is removed. Identify when contraction ends safe rollback and what forward repair remains. Reversing traffic does not reverse every stored or external effect.
Which duties remain with a team using a managed database? || Schema, access, retention, application transaction use, acknowledgment choices, and validation of recovery still require owners. Provider tasks vary by contract. Test maintenance, throttling, failover, and credential changes instead of assuming “managed” covers every application correctness need.
Scale-down kills a worker holding an unrecorded job. What was missing? || Durable accepted-work identity and a safe drain or transfer protocol. Stop new claims, persist progress, and let another valid owner recover as required. Killing a process cannot preserve work that existed only in its memory, regardless of how well the platform schedules replacements.
A feature is disabled after writing a new data format. What risk remains? || Old code and clients may still read the new state. A flag changes exposure, not stored compatibility or past effects. Keep migration and reader support explicit, including what rollback or forward repair is supported after the new data exists.
Why do two successfully scheduled replicas not necessarily form a correct shared service? || Each may use an unrelated local database or incompatible configuration. Placement establishes running instances, not common state ownership. Define the shared or coordinated data model, identity, and failure behaviour before distributing requests across them.''')
add(21, '''What must accompany a reusable benchmark result? || Workload, data distribution, hardware or capacity, versions, warmup, concurrency, duration, success definition, variability, and relevant failures. A function microbenchmark cannot establish full application throughput. Preserve enough setup to reproduce and interpret the claim rather than presenting one number without its conditions.
How can coordinated omission hide waiting? || A generator waits for a slow reply before issuing later work that independent real arrivals would already have submitted. Some queueing and delay disappear from the observed population. Choose a generator model matching the workload and record offered, completed, failed, and abandoned work.
Why is a short measured throughput maximum not a safe production target? || Real operation includes variance, maintenance, background work, and required failure capacity. Near saturation, extra arrivals sharply worsen waiting or rejection. Locate the useful knee and reserve margin against the actual objective rather than running indefinitely at one short benchmark's ceiling.
Why can cost per attempt hide increasing cost per useful outcome? || Retries and failures can enlarge the attempt denominator without producing additional completed orders or documents. Define successful business outcomes and the accounting period, while retaining technical breakdowns. Compare candidates with equivalent correctness and service objectives.
What should an architecture fitness function protect? || A meaningful required property such as module dependencies, tenant isolation, compatibility, or tested recovery. It should permit harmless implementation changes and be updated when a valid architecture decision changes the requirement. Its pass establishes only its covered evidence, not universal correctness.
An index fixes the slow query. Why might sharding have been a poorer first step? || Sharding changes ownership, routing, transactions, and migration while the avoidable scan may remain. A targeted implementation fix can address the measured mechanism with less risk. Architectural change is justified when the underlying limit actually requires it, not because it sounds more scalable.
What does killing a process only before work begins fail to test? || It misses gaps such as commit before reply, output write before completion record, or resumed work after lease expiry. Inject faults at the boundary in question and check both serving and required state. Successful restart alone does not establish that accepted work survived.
Source and destination counts match but tenant owners differ. Why did validation miss it? || A count compares quantity, not identity or meaning. Compare keys, versions, relationships, ownership, and business invariants as appropriate. State whether the check examines all records or samples. Migration, replay, and recovery need evidence that the right data reached the right scope.''')

THOUGHT={
2:'How would simultaneous retries change an estimate based on average user sessions?',
3:'What changes when a local dependency becomes a cross-region call?',
4:'Who should own accepted upload work after its initiating request is cancelled?',
5:'Which payment rules should remain the same when objects are replaced by pure functions?',
6:'How should resources be owned when a job outlives its request and user session?',
7:'What evidence would justify extracting payments into a separate service?',
8:'Can finite storage support a permanent promise to recognise every old idempotency key?',
9:'How should restoration from a historical backup respect later deletion decisions?',
10:'What changes when one local transaction is divided across independent databases?',
11:'When should a product stop serving rather than return a stale cached result?',
12:'Can more partitions scale one globally hot exclusive ownership decision?',
13:'How would you investigate an extracted field after both source and model have changed?',
14:'Which operations may continue during a partition while exclusive allocation must wait?',
15:'What must a repaired region establish before it accepts authoritative writes again?',
16:'How could one broker transaction be exactly-once while two emails are sent?',
17:'How can a disaster drill restore infrastructure yet fail the business recovery requirement?',
18:'What should revocation mean for an issued object capability and a queued export?',
19:'What evidence would support a latency explanation beyond correlation with a release?',
20:'How can rollback restore old code while leaving incompatible data or effects?',
21:'When could a more expensive architecture be simpler to operate overall?'}
THOUGHT_ANSWERS={
2:'Retries add attempts without adding new user intentions. Model their timing, maximum amplification, and the failing dependency separately from normal sessions. A correlated retry wave can exceed a peak factor based on independent arrivals. Bound attempts and admission, then test useful completion and recovery under that burst.',
3:'The call adds travel, connection, timeout, trust, and partial-failure behaviour. A local exception and an unknown remote outcome are different. Revisit deadlines, operation identity, encoding, credentials, and fallback, measuring the whole path. Preserve the business meaning while changing the communication arrangement.',
4:'If cancellation only ends the connection but the business still requires processing, a durable job or workflow must retain identity, input, and progress. Cancel and release request-owned resources without discarding that obligation. If the work itself should stop, persist the chosen cancellation and block inappropriate later completion.',
5:'Currency, units, allowed transitions, operation identity, and uncertainty remain business rules regardless of representation. Pure decision functions can make them easier to test. A separate effect interpreter still needs authorisation, transactions, provider identity, and reconciliation. Refactoring should preserve observed outcomes, not merely move methods into functions.',
6:'Give the job an explicit lifetime and scope, with durable identifiers rather than captured request objects. Create or borrow resources under a documented owner and close them at the appropriate boundary. Define whether current membership must be rechecked; a long-lived job must not silently inherit expired user authority.',
7:'Look for stable capability ownership, different scaling or isolation needs, and an operating team prepared for network and version failures. Compare those benefits with added data coordination and recovery. Preserve existing payment invariants and histories through migration. A separate deployment is useful only when the required independence can actually be exercised.',
8:'Not for an unbounded set of arbitrary distinct operations unless another constraint supplies a bound or persistent evidence elsewhere. Define key retention, expiry, reuse, and client expectations. A permanent business identity may require a durable record rather than an expiring cache. Make the promise fit the information the system can retain.',
9:'Restore into a controlled path, apply relevant deletion decisions, and reconcile raw and derived copies before serving. Rebuild indexes from the permitted current state rather than blindly expose an older snapshot. Keep deletion evidence narrow and purpose-limited. The backup and live-data lifecycle need a combined recovery procedure.',
10:'A former all-or-nothing invariant may no longer share one commit boundary. Identify which related changes require agreement, whether a distributed transaction is justified, or whether a workflow with explicit intermediate states and compensation fits. Local success at each store does not guarantee the combined business outcome.',
11:'Stop when the operation requires current authority or the permitted stale interval has ended. An old description may be allowed, while allocating scarce stock or deciding current access may not be. State which actions degrade and which wait or reject, with visible status and a recovery path.',
12:'Cold partitions do not divide the authority required for one exclusive key. Read copies or batching can help suitable work, while changing the invariant or allocation model needs a valid protocol. Measure the hot owner and explain how any proposed partition changes its actual work without permitting conflicting winners.',
13:'Use stable references to the exact source version, region, transform/model version, parameters, and review evidence. Keep the disputed output version identifiable. Re-running a changed or nondeterministic model is not proof of the old result. Retain appropriate evidence under the lifecycle policy and distinguish reproducibility limits from missing provenance.',
14:'Operations with explicitly permitted stale or independent results may continue. Exclusive allocation needs valid authority and may have to wait or reject when agreement is unavailable. State per-operation observations rather than weaken the entire system silently. Recovery must reconcile progress without introducing another owner.',
15:'It needs current authority, compatible committed history, required durable copies, valid routing, and sufficient capacity. An old leader may require rebuilding and fencing. Apply the supported rejoin protocol and reconcile uncertain external effects. A repaired machine being reachable is not evidence that its old state is safe to resume.',
16:'The broker may atomically couple consumed progress and produced records while email lies outside that transaction. A consumer can send, lose its completion reply, and send again on recovery. Use the provider contract, stable identity where supported, and reconciliation; do not extend the broker guarantee to an unrelated effect.',
17:'Machines can restart while the recovered data is too old, encrypted backups are unreadable, active authority conflicts, or accepted jobs are missing. Check the complete business journey and its RTO/RPO, including keys, state, identity, validation, and useful traffic return. A drill proves only the scenarios it actually exercises.',
18:'Specify both boundaries. An already issued capability may remain valid until expiry unless the delivery system supports revocation. A queued export may need a current policy check before execution or publication. Keep users informed about the chosen lifetime and ensure workers cannot bypass it using an old submission snapshot.',
19:'Locate the extra wait or execution on affected paths, compare normal and slow populations, inspect resource saturation and changed behaviour, and test a focused intervention. A release marker is a clue. Evidence is stronger when the mechanism predicts the symptom and the correction changes that mechanism under comparable conditions.',
20:'Old code can return while new schemas, values, provider calls, and published messages remain. Maintain compatibility during overlap and identify the point where contraction ends safe rollback. Some outcomes need forward repair or compensation. Restoring an artifact cannot reverse every state transition it previously performed.',
21:'Compare total work for people as well as the invoice. A managed service may reduce patching, backup, or recovery burden while adding charges and leaving application responsibilities. Evaluate the same correctness and service goals with explicit staffing assumptions. Choose the design the team can understand, change, and recover reliably.'}

TASKS={
2:('Implement a capacity worksheet with explicit user activity, peak, and per-instance inputs.','The default calculation should produce about 5,787.04 average RPS and 28,935.19 peak RPS. At an assumed 600 RPS capacity and 60% target, round up to 81 active instances and 82 for one-instance loss. Validate input ranges and keep database, network, and failure-domain feasibility separate from this arithmetic.'),
3:('Implement a cold/warm request calculator and check invalid stage times.','Use 20+40+40+55 = 155 ms for the assumed cold path. A reusable setup can leave the same 55 ms request/work stage. Remove only stages actually reused, reject negative inputs, and label this as an arithmetic model rather than a protocol benchmark.'),
4:('Reproduce a lost counter update, then build a bounded upload-work model.','Show A and B both reading zero and each writing one. Protect their whole shared read–modify–write step. Bound active workers and waiting references separately. Model I/O waits differently from CPU service demand, and identify what accepted-work state must persist if a process-local queue disappears.'),
5:('Replace hardcoded payment-provider construction with an injected recording gateway.','Exercise approved, declined, and uncertain results without real charges. Preserve currency and minor units and check resulting business state. A fake helps test orchestration; real encoding, persistence, and provider recovery need separate evidence. Do not turn an uncertain timeout into a decline to simplify the test.'),
6:('Demonstrate transient, scoped, and singleton identities across two request scopes.','A scoped object should be A,A in scope one and B,B in scope two. Transients should differ; the same process singleton should remain S. Add an owned disposable resource and check scope cleanup. Demonstrate why retaining it in a singleton breaks the intended lifetime.'),
7:('Implement shopping modules and one meaningful dependency-boundary check.','Let orders use a payment contract without importing private payment storage or writing its tables. Check that boundary, then replace the provider adapter while preserving the order rule. Avoid a test tied to every internal class name; protect the dependency whose violation would spread provider details or bypass ownership.'),
8:('Implement keyset continuation and a durable operation-key model for synthetic payments.','Use a stable ordered pair for continuation and test an insertion before a page boundary. Bind actor, key, fingerprint, progress, and result under appropriate atomicity. Identical retries return the old operation; changed payloads conflict. A dictionary lookup followed by an unrelated insert does not protect concurrent or restarted callers.'),
9:('Model profiles, financial entries, chat history, events, and PDFs from their required operations.','List access patterns, rules, identity, retention, and recoverability before choosing stores. Several needs may share an engine. Keep immutable bytes distinguishable from metadata and derived search results. Explain how each copy is rebuilt or recovered, rather than assigning one product family per data type without a reason.'),
10:('Run the conditional last-seat claim with two connections to one store.','Check that one claimant succeeds and another receives conflict. Identify the transaction protecting seat ownership, then test rollback or interruption at the relevant boundary. Keep payment and expiry as separate workflow concerns: a successful local claim does not atomically perform an external charge.'),
11:('Run ideal LRU, then reproduce stale cache-aside population after invalidation.','A,B,C,A,D at capacity three evicts B. Next pause a reader with version one, commit version two and invalidate, then resume old population. Show the stale result. Apply a justified version or coordination rule if the freshness requirement forbids it; shortening TTL alone does not remove the race.'),
12:('Compare rendezvous owners before and after adding D, then introduce a hot key.','In the provided model, each changed assignment moves to D. Count key distribution, then send most requests to one key and measure traffic distribution separately. Adding cold owners does not divide that key. State what safe serving or ownership change would address its actual pressure.'),
13:('Implement source-version and attempt checks, then plan a safe index-generation switch.','Reject old epochs and wrong inputs with Workflow. Preserve transform and source identity. Build the new representation from a defined position, include later updates and deletes, validate, then switch. Counts alone cannot show correct versions, content, or access scope.'),
14:('Evaluate Lamport updates, vector relationships, and fixed-quorum intersection.','lamport_receive(5,9) gives 10. Vectors [1,0] and [0,1] are concurrent; [1,0] precedes [1,2] under this comparison. With fixed N=3,R=2,W=2, sets intersect. Explain the additional ordering, persistence, and membership rules needed for any larger consistency claim.'),
15:('Implement a receiver that rejects stale attempt epochs.','After epoch 8 owns the local workflow, a completion at epoch 7 must fail. Perform comparison and update atomically in a persistent version. Then identify every external effect receiver that also needs authority enforcement; a client-side lease flag cannot stop a paused process from resuming elsewhere.'),
16:('Crash the outbox relay after publication, then process the duplicate with a local inbox transaction.','The fixture should show two deliveries and one counter change. Event identity and the local effect must commit together. Replace the counter conceptually with email and identify the missing external protocol; local duplicate recognition alone cannot establish one external send across the failure gap.'),
17:('Calculate nested retry amplification and design a bounded provider-outage policy.','Three attempts at four layers permit up to 81 downstream attempts. Introduce an overall budget, deadlines, admission, and appropriate isolation. Choose a valid pending or failure response instead of fabricated payment approval. Check recovery and remaining uncertain operations after the provider becomes reachable.'),
18:('Test the synthetic tenant predicate, then trace all data paths using that decision.','permit_demo should allow the intended same-tenant action and deny tenant A access to B. It is not credential validation. Test scope in caches, jobs, objects, exports, and search too. A valid predicate cannot protect a boundary that never calls it or receives untrusted identity context.'),
19:('Build the five-second trace model and an asynchronous completion indicator.','The fixture has 4.6 seconds of pool waiting, 100 ms query, and 300 ms elsewhere. Pool wait is 92% of elapsed time. Investigate its cause using population and resource evidence. For jobs, measure submission to required outcome, not just HTTP acceptance. Keep detailed identifiers out of unbounded metric dimensions.'),
20:('Plan and test an expand-and-contract release with old and new readers.','Add compatible state, deploy overlap-safe readers, backfill with bounded progress, and verify. Retire old readers before removing obsolete schema. Test rollback before that point and identify what requires forward repair afterward. Expose representative traffic to a canary, including important expensive paths.'),
21:('Compare the three fictional architectures against equal requirements and costs.','cost_options totals 371, 551, and 1,402 monthly units. Keep these inputs separate from measured capacity and reliability. Use the constructed latency and independent-component examples only within their assumptions, then state which real tests would establish each candidate meets the original workload and service goals.')}

DESIGN={
2:('A team sizes servers from daily average RPS alone. Identify the missing evidence and propose a safer estimation process.','Add operation mix, peak duration, storage and transfer work, tested per-instance capacity, utilisation, and surviving failure capacity. Keep each assumption explicit. An estimate becomes a test workload; it is not itself proof that the whole architecture meets latency or recovery needs.'),
3:('A payment client retries every timeout as a new request. Trace a duplicate effect and repair the contract.','Let the provider commit, lose the reply, then receive the new request. Use stable operation identity and a supported status or retry path. Keep total waiting bounded, while treating timeout as unknown outcome rather than evidence of nonexecution.'),
4:('A server starts an unbounded background task for each upload. Explain its overload and crash behaviour, then redesign acceptance.','Queued buffers and open resources can grow without bound, and process loss destroys unrecorded accepted work. Bound active and waiting jobs separately, persist required identity and input references, and define rejection or waiting. Choose CPU parallelism and I/O concurrency according to measured service demand.'),
5:('A payment class mixes fee rules, HTTP parsing, provider calls, and storage. Plan a behaviour-preserving refactor.','Capture approval, decline, uncertainty, and storage-error outcomes. Separate transport, rule calculation, provider mapping, and persistence in small steps. Keep contracts explicit and test outcomes. Structural improvement must preserve amounts and recovery meaning rather than merely move code into more classes.'),
6:('A singleton resolves and stores a request connection from a global container. Find the hidden dependency and lifetime failure.','The lookup conceals what the singleton needs, and the stored resource may expire at request end. Supply an appropriate per-operation dependency or narrow factory with explicit ownership. Do not repair the graph merely by extending every connection lifetime indefinitely.'),
7:('Several services share tables and require simultaneous releases. Review whether the deployment split is useful.','Map who owns decisions and data, what changes together, and what fails together. A modular consolidation may be simpler, or revised contracts and ownership may justify separation. Compare independent operation with the actual network, migration, and incident costs.'),
8:('A payment API returns a Boolean and provides no operation status. Redesign it for an uncertain provider reply.','Create a stable intent identity, explicit pending/approved/declined/uncertain semantics, validated money units, and scoped repeat-key handling. Offer status recovery. Define compatibility and permission as well as field shape so clients know what repeating the request means.'),
9:('A search engine holds the only copy of private document text. Review ownership, recovery, and deletion.','Keep a recoverable source and exact identity/version relationships for derived entries. Enforce access while indexing lags, and track deletion across copies and restores. If search truly owns the authoritative data, it needs the corresponding complete durability and recovery contract rather than assumptions borrowed from a projection.'),
10:('Two doctors can each go off call after seeing the other available. Propose a transaction design preserving at least one on call.','Protect the multi-row predicate using suitable serialization, locking, or a shared conflict point. Individual row versions alone may not conflict. Handle whole-transaction retries with fresh reads, and keep externally repeated effects outside an unsafe retry body.'),
11:('A cache-aside reader repopulates old data after the writer invalidates it. Define and repair the actual freshness requirement.','Trace old read, new commit, invalidation, delayed old insertion. If that stale result is forbidden, use a supported version or coordination protocol. If bounded staleness is allowed, state its limit and validate authoritative state for critical actions. TTL is not a universal repair.'),
12:('Scaling application replicas increases database timeouts. Design an investigation and correction.','Measure aggregate connections, hold and lock times, offered work, useful completion, and queues. Bound admission and preserve recovery capacity. Improve the constrained query or resource where evidence supports it. More upstream capacity can be harmful when it only amplifies demand on the same owner.'),
13:('A reindex copies records while updates and deletions continue. Design the handoff to a new serving generation.','Choose a defined snapshot/change position, capture concurrent changes including deletes, validate versions and access, and switch through an explicit pointer or epoch. Keep rollback compatible. Compare meaning and identities, not just counts, and retain appropriate rebuild evidence.'),
14:('During a partition, two sides both try to allocate the same exclusive resource. State an acceptable serving policy.','Identify the authority protocol and which side can establish a valid decision. Reject or wait where authority is unavailable; permit only explicitly safe weaker operations. A timeout does not stop the old actor. Recovery needs evidence and fencing rather than merging two exclusive winners.'),
15:('A paused worker returns after its lease expired and a new owner started. Design stale-effect protection.','Use ordered ownership tokens checked atomically where protected effects are accepted. Verify owner identity on release as well. Distinguish earlier accepted effects from later stale actions. The lease detects an interval; receiver enforcement prevents the resumed old owner from writing under obsolete authority.'),
16:('An order commits but its event is never published. Design durable publication and repeated-delivery handling.','Commit the order and outbox intent together. Retry relay progress with a stable event ID, accepting that publish-before-mark can repeat. A consumer protects its local effect with the event identity in one transaction. Other external effects need their own identity and reconciliation contract.'),
17:('A provider slowdown holds every connection and triggers layered retries. Break the cascade without inventing business success.','Use overall deadlines, bounded admission and retry budgets, resource isolation, and limited recovery probes. Preserve a valid pending or degraded response where allowed. Track unresolved effects, and reserve capacity for reconciliation and recovery. Test the fallback path for its own overload risk.'),
18:('A private export is authorised at submission but its worker trusts the old tenant field forever. Review the complete journey.','Carry trusted identity and resource scope, apply the chosen current revocation rule, limit worker capabilities, and protect the output/download lifetime. Trace caches, search, and audit too. Test membership removal between enqueue, execution, publication, and download under the stated policy.'),
19:('HTTP acceptance stays fast while background jobs stop finishing. Redesign the service indicators and alert.','Define eligible accepted jobs, required terminal outcomes, and age limits. Track stage wait, attempts, stuck work, and useful completion through stable identity. Alert on actionable oldest-age or completion harm. A healthy endpoint or enqueue rate covers only the beginning of the journey.'),
20:('A blue-green release shares one database and removes a column needed by the old version. Review its rollback plan.','Parallel app environments do not make shared state reversible. Use compatible expansion and overlap-safe readers, backfill and verify, then retire old readers before contraction. Mark the end of safe rollback and the forward repair path. Test representative mixed-version traffic.'),
21:('A faster and cheaper benchmark configuration omits failures and disables authorisation. Can it replace the original design?','It tests a different workload and service contract. Restore mandatory correctness and access checks, include failed and waiting work, disclose setup, and compare useful outcomes under the original requirements. A favourable number cannot compensate for removing the properties the service must provide.')}

CASE_ASSESSMENT=[
('conceptual','C1','Why can a click count and a seat claim use different consistency rules?','A click count can often tolerate delayed aggregation. A seat claim needs one valid owner before confirmation. State each operation’s rule and failure behaviour rather than assigning one vague consistency label to the whole application.'),
('conceptual','C2','What does a persisted chat receipt establish, and what does it not establish?','It establishes the accepted history state under the storage contract. It does not prove a device received a frame or a person read it. Track those later acknowledgments separately, with reconnect and deduplication behaviour for missing or repeated frames.'),
('conceptual','C3','Why can a scheduler lease not guarantee one external execution?','An old worker can pause past expiry, then resume beside a replacement. Conditional local completion protects local progress, while the external receiver needs safe identity or enforced fencing. Some effects accepted before authority changed still need reconciliation.'),
('conceptual','C4','Why is equal final text insufficient to validate a collaborative editor?','The history might include unauthorised edits, invalid rich-text structure, wrong Unicode handling, or broken undo semantics. Check the selected protocol’s convergence and supported context, then test access, structure, and recovery independently. A matching string proves only that particular final comparison.'),
('conceptual','C5','What should complete mean for a document-processing workflow?','It must name the required usable, permitted output and its exact source, pipeline, and review evidence. A worker finishing extraction may leave publication or review unresolved. Track those stages separately and preserve provenance instead of treating one exception-free attempt as verified truth.'),
('scenario','S1','Payment succeeds after a seat hold expires. What may the service safely do?','Use the documented recovery policy. It may reacquire the seat only if current ownership permits it, or follow a refund/other supported resolution. It must not issue against another buyer’s confirmed seat. Preserve provider identity and reconcile the financial outcome rather than discard it.'),
('scenario','S2','The provider charges, the process stops, and the client retries. How is another logical charge avoided?','Keep stable intent and provider operation identity. Use the provider’s safe repeat or status protocol and reconcile the existing effect before new submission. Apply verified evidence to one valid local transition and posting set. Local keys alone cannot impose unsupported behaviour on the provider.'),
('scenario','S3','An old extraction worker finishes after document deletion. What stops publication?','The authoritative completion transition checks current epoch, input, and live deletion state atomically. The publisher and derived paths must respect that state too. Cancelling the worker alone is insufficient because it may already be delayed or running elsewhere.'),
('design','D1','Review a short-link service at 100,000 redirects/s after its cache becomes unavailable.','The warm 90% hit assumption no longer limits source traffic to 10,000 reads/s. Define bounded origin admission or an allowed failure response, test cold capacity, and preserve revocation rules. More cache replicas alone do not establish safe recovery or immediate invalidation.'),
('implementation','I1','Choose one case and implement its failure exercise with a checked invariant.','Name the exact local boundary and expected outcomes, inject the described interruption, and compare the recovered state. Include repetition or stale work where relevant. Use the mechanism fixtures as examples, while keeping external guarantees within the actual provider or protocol contract.'),
('thought_experiment','T1','How would you choose between another region, tested restores, and stronger reconciliation?','Start with the most harmful unmet requirement and demonstrated failure. Another region cannot recover missing operation identity, and retries cannot restore a destroyed sole copy. Compare risk reduction, operating work, and the evidence each option can produce. Record the choice and the condition for reconsidering it.')]
